WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.760
- I'm gonna keep my
introductory comments very brief

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.450
so we can get straight into the panel.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:12.930
But I just wanted to say a word or two

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.853
about how this panel came about.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.560
Basically this panel emerged

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.453
because I have a very messy desk.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.110
I've been accumulating all
these books on Vietnam studies

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.780
in Southeast Asian Studies.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.390
And one day as these books are piling up

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:31.500
on my desk, I noticed
an accidental pattern

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.070
pop out from the spines of the books

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.840
that are piled up in my quarantine office,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.230
also known here in the Harms
family as the dinner table.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.330
And I noticed in this pattern
that I was the proud owner

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.620
of three recently published books

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.340
all on the subject of
militarized landscapes

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.260
of the Indochina conflicts.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.310
And they were based on
research in Laos, Vietnam,

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:54.580
and Cambodia.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:55.920
And I immediately wondered,

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.210
when I saw this pattern emerge,

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.260
I wondered if the authors
were in conversation

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.093
with each other.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.970
So then a few emails later,

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.660
I wrote to the authors and I wondered

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.220
if they were in conversation.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:07.910
And they said they'd love to be.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.100
So all of a sudden we had a panel.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.940
And now here we are with a crowd.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.330
Actually, let me see we
have a crowd of 45 people

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.933
that emerged out of just
this mess on my desk.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.610
So let me just take a moment
to introduce the speakers.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.580
And I think just by
naming them and the titles

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.930
of their books, you can
imagine the connections

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.090
that I think will be emerging
from today's discussion.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:32.740
First is David Biggs.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.280
He's a professor of
history at UC Riverside

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.750
he is the author of "Footprints of War,

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.340
Militarized Landscapes in Vietnam,"

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.530
published by the University

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.980
of Washington press, in 2018.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.230
Next is Jonathan Padwe.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.480
He's an associate
professor of anthropology

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.770
at the University of Hawaii Manoa.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:55.630
His book is called "Disturbed
Forest Fragmented Memories

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:59.160
Jarai and Other Lives in
the Cambodian Highlands,"

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.520
which was also published by
the University of Washington

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.570
press in 2020.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:07.490
And we also have Leah
Zani an anthropologist,

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.500
author and poet who holds a PhD

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.110
from UC Irvine,

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.740
and who is the author of "Bomb Children.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.240
Life in the Former Battlefields of Laos,"

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.123
published by Duke
University Press in 2019.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.110
As you can see the titles of these books

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:24.530
immediately form a pattern.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.910
So I won't go into any detail

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.820
about what the goal of
today's discussion is, really,

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.930
the main goal is to bring
these authors into conversation

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.090
with each other.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:37.270
So the panel is designed
specifically to allow the authors

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.440
to engage in an open discussion.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:40.990
And I'll serve as the moderator,

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:44.000
but I'm hoping that the
panel will really evolve

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.970
into an open discussion
between the authors

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.433
and all the audience members.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.620
So to get us started, I'll
start with two questions.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.940
The first question will be to ask them

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.930
in five minutes or less just to tell us

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.720
as an audience especially for those of you

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.450
who haven't read the books yet.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.220
Just give a sense of what the books

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.210
are about what the main argument is there,

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.300
any innovations they feel are in it?

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:08.610
And then my second question,

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.200
after they've all talked
about the kind of main project

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.080
of the book will be to ask each of them

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.240
to pick a kind of favorite
passage of the book.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.670
One of the things that's
really significant about all

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:20.910
of these books is the
way they've attended,

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.680
not only to the topic of
militarized landscapes,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.020
but to the art and practice of writing.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.140
So I thought it'd be really great

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.330
to kind of treat this a
little bit like a miniature

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.980
book reading as well.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.660
So that will be my second question.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.010
And then after that,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.860
that should probably take about 30 minutes

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:38.950
to do all of those,

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:41.360
and then I'll open it up to questions

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.700
from the open Zoom audience.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.830
So let me turn to the first speaker,

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:52.120
David Biggs tell us David
in five minutes or less,

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.470
what is your book about?

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:56.270
And what do you see as the
main argument for innovation?

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:01.510
- Well, thanks, Erik, and
colleagues, panelists,

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:03.100
everybody for coming.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.730
It's really a pleasure
to be here in this forum.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:10.270
This forum is better than no
forum at all. (David laughing)

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:14.210
And Erik, I wonder if you can
just hit play on this video,

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:16.460
and I'll use it to talk
about the first part

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:18.960
and then the other slides
are for the second part.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:24.800
So this is a clip from
WGBH's Vietnam series.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.650
It was filmed in 1981.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:31.630
On the road from the Hue
Phu Bai airport into Hue,

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:36.120
and it is a panorama of
what used to be camp Eagle,

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.330
this was 101st airborne divisions camp

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:43.593
really operated from 1968 to 72.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.830
And what you're seeing is six years later,

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:52.630
the residual detritus and
debris that's left from this.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:56.450
And the US forces pulled
out in about one month

00:04:56.450 --> 00:05:01.450
they went from 15,000 troops
to about 400 caretakers.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.340
And what interests me in
this project on militarized

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.930
landscape is really
thinking about the scale

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.720
of the military occupation in Vietnam.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.850
We hear a lot about the bombing,

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:16.740
intense bombing in a very small area.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.190
But the question I had is really

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:21.950
how does the country
recover from this kind

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:23.898
of military footprint?

00:05:23.898 --> 00:05:27.350
And that's where the term
footprints of war comes from.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:29.193
It's sort of thinking about that.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.870
And theoretically, I'm very much inspired

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:37.800
by a kind of dialectical
approach to military footprints

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.060
and militarization.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.950
And I found the work of Andrea Fab

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.250
who is completely incomprehensible,

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.927
but wonderful nonetheless,
very interesting,

00:05:46.927 --> 00:05:49.100
and that he talks about his native Paris.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:52.730
And he talks about a certain
dialectic and urban spaces

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:57.360
between representational
spaces, spaces of practice,

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.450
sort of physical creations,

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:01.900
and how these interact.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.350
And he's basically talking
about a certain agency of space.

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:08.410
And I tried to apply that in my book

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:10.750
to militarize spaces.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:15.150
And the sort of question
that really drives this book

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:20.150
is thinking about militarized
spaces as having a kind

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:25.160
of agency on development on post war.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:27.170
But as I began to study these places,

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:29.220
I began to realize that, in fact,

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.770
these same military spaces
had been occupied before

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:37.140
and they have been occupied
actually by military forces

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.100
for several centuries.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.890
So the book that unfolds
is really a long history

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:44.333
of militarization.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.630
It's contextualizing
the American occupation,

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:53.550
in a much longer dialogue that
Vietnamese people have had

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:57.830
with different military
forces in these landscapes.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:02.090
And so I use landscape as
a kind of analytical lens

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:05.780
to look at these
dialectical relationships.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:08.363
So that's kind of what
I'm trying to do there.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.383
- Great, thanks David.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.920
Leah, I ask if you can go next.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:19.070
Answer the same question,

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:20.280
what is your book about?

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.270
And what do you think are
some of your main innovations,

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.733
and allow me just to
unshare the screen briefly?

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:35.900
- Well, I think any book
has multiple origins.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.910
But one origin story for this book

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.230
that I'd like to share with you today

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:44.030
has to do with these jars of bits of bombs

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:47.180
that I brought back from my field studies

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.770
with explosive clearance teams.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:53.810
And at first I started
collecting these by accident,

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:56.380
I would just find them
when I was out walking

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:58.650
with bomb technicians.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:01.920
And they would say, "oh,
that's a bit of a bomb,"

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:03.400
and then they would
examine it and make sure

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:04.840
it wasn't explosive.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:06.879
And then I was allowed to take it home

00:08:06.879 --> 00:08:08.480
(Leah laughing) with me if I wanted to.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.980
So I started collecting
these sort of by accident.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:16.900
But it slowly became this
kind of paranoid process,

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:22.370
where every time I saw a
bit of metal on the ground,

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:25.533
I would think to myself,
is that part of a bomb.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.360
And there was one time when I was out

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.780
with a bomb technician in a village.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:33.750
And we found this piece of metal here,

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:34.670
which you can't quite see,

00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:36.713
but I'll tell you about it in my story.

00:08:37.552 --> 00:08:39.200
I found this piece of metal in a village.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.377
And she looks at it and she says,

00:08:41.377 --> 00:08:43.247
"oh, this is part of a rocket."

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:45.340
And she hands it to me,

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:47.470
and she says "you can keep it."

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:52.470
And as I was looking
at this piece of metal,

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.470
I became suspicious because I didn't think

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.020
it was actually part of a bomb.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:00.931
But she had just told me that it was

00:09:00.931 --> 00:09:05.931
and so I kept on kind of
fiddling with this item,

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:09.360
and asking other people to look at it,

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:13.000
and eventually realizing that
it wasn't part of a rocket.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:14.793
It was part of a motorcycle engine.

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:21.150
And so I saved it in my bomb collection,

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.750
even though it's not part
of a bomb, (Leah laughing)

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:28.750
as a reminder, that it's
really easy to mistake the war

00:09:31.996 --> 00:09:34.030
like the most important
or most visible thing

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:36.180
that's happening in these battlefields.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.600
But that's often not the case

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:43.760
that we have to pay
attention to the daily lives

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:47.400
that are being built
on top of or alongside

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:48.360
these remains of war,

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:49.700
like what David just showed us

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:53.290
in those compelling archival footage.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:56.870
And so I kept that piece
of a motorcycle engine

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:59.110
to remind myself to really honor

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:01.050
and pay attention to
the lives of the people

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:04.710
that are currently living
in these old battlefields

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:08.233
and to not let the war
overshadow my senses.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:11.560
And so the book in a way is kind

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:15.440
of a larger response to that.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:18.480
What seems to me to be a kind
of ethical humanist impulse

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.410
to really respect the lives of the people

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.013
that are currently living in these zones.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:28.150
And I developed this idea of parallelism,

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:30.840
that it's not that one is
more important than the other,

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.550
but that these two experiences one of war

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:34.830
and one of everyday life

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:39.410
need to be treated side by
side and given equal relevance

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:44.260
in an anthropological
or historical analysis.

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:47.073
And that's harder to do than it sounds.

00:10:47.957 --> 00:10:51.250
And the book is a kind of
experimental ethnography

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:53.973
of what that might look like in practice.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:57.930
Yes, and so I look forward

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:00.540
to sharing a little exert later

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.890
of what I imagined that
looking like in practice,

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:05.973
when we get to that, thank you.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.750
- Great, thank you, Leah.

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:12.960
I'm gonna turn it over to Jonathan,

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:16.110
to talk briefly about
what he sees his book

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:17.500
being about and what he sees

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:19.500
as the main contribution.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:22.220
Jonathan also asked me to share a screen.

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:24.493
So one moment as I do that.

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:29.753
Jonathan, can you see the slide?

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:31.833
- I can see it.

00:11:31.833 --> 00:11:32.990
I think we all can.

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:33.823
- [Eric] Great.

00:11:33.823 --> 00:11:37.170
- Yeah so this picture is just to give you

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:40.140
a little bit of geographical grounding

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:43.840
for where the work for
this book took place.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:46.550
And this is in the kind of
crook of the say, Sand River,

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:51.550
right where the Vietnam
border is with Cambodia.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.580
So the villages that I was working in

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:55.880
are right inside of that border.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:57.770
You can switch forward there, Eric,

00:11:57.770 --> 00:11:59.730
I just wanted to sort of locate us.

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:03.250
But then this next image is just for you

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:05.650
to see a landscape that's
a swidden landscape

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:07.200
up in the hills of Cambodia.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.430
And you can immediately
see that there's a story

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:15.010
of living in the land
that the swidden process,

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:20.010
this process of burning,
clearing, planting,

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:26.820
plots of farmland creates
in the landscape itself,

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:30.910
there's a history of living
and it's a moving history

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:33.080
as the swidden field itself sort of moves

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:34.373
across the landscape.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:39.370
The book, "Disturbed
Forests, Fragmented Memories"

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:42.870
is an effort to narrate a series

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:46.260
of historical conjectures
that the Jarai people

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:49.500
of Northeast Cambodia have experienced,

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.850
from their perspective,

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:54.500
and from the perspective
of the landscape itself.

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:57.840
The idea is that the
landscape tells stories

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:00.550
certainly for the Jarai people,

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:02.260
when you are hanging out with them

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:03.940
and talking about history,

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.250
talking about the past.

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:10.250
It is always in reference to
the places that they live,

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:14.320
the places that historical
events have taken place,

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:18.120
and the forests, the rivers,

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:20.890
the components of the landscape itself,

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:22.960
play a role often,

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:24.960
and the spirits that inhabit these places

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:26.480
play a role in these stories.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:29.880
They shape the stories
they are, in a way agentive

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.803
they're actors in these stories.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:36.340
And the thrust of the book,

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.590
the idea is that these stories provide

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:42.080
new insights into regional
and world history,

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:46.410
that these are subaltern
histories that reimagine

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:49.690
and often contradict
conventional narratives

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:52.540
about the experience of
colonialism, for example,

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:54.000
about the Vietnam War

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.340
about the Cambodian genocide.

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:59.637
And I note in the book
that E.P. Thompson called

00:13:59.637 --> 00:14:02.860
"This Kind of Account History From Below."

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.740
And I make the point that this
is elevationally inaccurate

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:08.020
in the case of the Jarai
since they are highlanders,

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:09.500
and that we should call these histories

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:11.013
from the hills instead.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:15.140
There's a theory of landscape

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:17.963
underlying the approach in the book.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:24.240
The landscapes are both
observed and lived in

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:29.830
and they bring together at
least three fields of inquiry

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:33.010
that have been important to
anthropologists and historians

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:38.010
and geographers, ecology, representation

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:39.550
and materiality, right.

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:42.150
So by focusing on landscape,

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:45.727
we're able to look at the actual ecology

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:50.996
that people live in,

00:14:50.996 --> 00:14:52.240
and this could be in an urban setting

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:56.433
as well as in a setting
like the Jarai hill country.

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:00.380
There's a representational
dimension to landscape,

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:03.520
landscapes are imbued with
meanings through the processes

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:05.670
of being lived in.

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:06.950
And at the same time,

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:09.630
there's an opening here
for understanding them

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:12.760
in the materiality of the landscape,

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:17.760
and also in a way that it is
active in the social world

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.723
of the people who inhabit it.

00:15:23.110 --> 00:15:25.960
There's one more point it was interesting,

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:27.320
I was trying to find the point

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:29.250
where I must have written
this down in the introduction

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:34.250
of my book, the role of
thinking with conjuncture

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:37.870
in putting together an
understanding of how we might

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:41.803
look at these landscapes
of all of this region.

00:15:43.168 --> 00:15:44.250
And I realized, of course,

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:47.860
that I just completely
subsumed this critical point

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:51.740
within myself and it comes
out in all of the different

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:52.573
pieces of the book,

00:15:52.573 --> 00:15:53.594
but it's never explicit.

00:15:53.594 --> 00:15:55.480
And this is this notion

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:58.070
I was really interested in the way

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:00.823
that scholars like Tania Li,

00:16:03.929 --> 00:16:06.350
talks about the conjunctural analysis

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:09.220
in her book "Land's End."

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:13.080
And other scholars also, Nancy
Palooza has a great article

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:16.180
that uses the concept of
conjuncture as an insight

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:20.320
as a way of getting insight
into historical processes.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.470
And of course, they're
borrowing from Stuart Hall

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:24.253
and Doreen Massey and others.

00:16:26.173 --> 00:16:30.880
And the idea here is that
people live in conversation

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:32.580
with the landscape that they inhabit

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:35.760
that they are constantly
interacting with it.

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:40.460
And those interactions
are shaped by the set

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:44.400
of political and economic
forces that are operating

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:45.390
in a given moment.

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:49.050
And these change over time, very abruptly.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:50.740
So even though it may
seem like there's this

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:54.500
sort of story of resilience,
with farming up in the hills,

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:56.270
when you really talk to people up there

00:16:56.270 --> 00:16:58.160
about what their experience has been

00:16:58.160 --> 00:16:58.993
of the past,

00:16:58.993 --> 00:17:01.710
you see that they've changed
their agricultural system

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:04.050
and their way of interacting
with their environment.

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:07.410
Or they've had it changed
on them very rapidly

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:10.470
over the course of these
different historical periods.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:13.640
So that what they were doing
during the colonial period,

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:15.720
and their relationship with the landscape

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:19.640
had salient features during say the moment

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:23.050
from 1952 or three, whenever
it was that Cambodia

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:25.130
gained independence.

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:28.050
It changes again with the arrival

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:32.600
of the Khmer Rouge, et cetera, right.

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:36.130
And it's not a surprise
that the landscape then

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:39.150
records and takes on
elements of this history

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:42.830
that it expresses the
historical conjuncture

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:47.120
that it is formed during and
these expressions live on

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:52.820
after that moment has
passed and serve as ways

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:54.780
into the past.

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:56.560
But only in a partial way, right?

00:17:56.560 --> 00:18:01.430
Because the ecologies are
constantly being reworked.

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:05.140
So this is this notion of the disturbance,

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:08.150
but also the disturbed force
and fragmented histories

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:10.080
that these histories are never complete.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:13.330
They're always partial.

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:14.580
So I'll leave it at that.

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:22.000
- Fantastic, Jonathan.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:23.710
So thanks to all three of you for sharing

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:27.230
a kind of introductory window

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:30.213
into these three fantastic manuscripts.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:32.640
A couple of things.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:35.960
Later, I will wanna ask you a question

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:38.850
about this idea of reading the landscape

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:40.400
as a historical archive,

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:41.840
cause I think that's
something that all three

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:43.730
of you can talk about.

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:46.190
But I'll save that question for later.

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:48.380
I wanna turn to something
that Leah mentioned

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:50.600
in passing when she was
introducing her book.

00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:54.160
She used the term
ethical humanist impulse.

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:57.820
And one of the things that's very evident

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:00.370
in all three of your
books is a deep attention

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:02.950
to the writerly practice,

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:05.050
narrative and storytelling.

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:07.400
And bringing in the story of a landscape

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:10.160
with actual narrative
strategies in the writing

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:12.340
of your text themselves.

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:16.303
Which I see is entangled with
that ethical humanist impulse.

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:19.640
So what I'd like to do
now is ask each of you

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:22.630
to pick a passage or a story,

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:23.940
you can either read from the book

00:19:23.940 --> 00:19:28.940
or retell a story, in your own
words from the book itself.

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:31.390
As a way to kind of give a window

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:34.120
to our audience into your approach,

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:36.293
your writerly approach to addressing this.

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:38.823
I'll start with Leah.

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:41.183
- Thank you, Eric.

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:47.880
Well the section I wanna
share with with you today

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:51.390
is the opening section of the book.

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:55.980
Which is a place where I
try to lay out narratively

00:19:57.510 --> 00:20:00.890
this concept of parallelism
and what it looks like

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:02.823
in the ethnographic record.

00:20:04.010 --> 00:20:05.370
And then I'll share a poem

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:08.863
that I wrote based off
of a similar encounter.

00:20:10.857 --> 00:20:12.193
"The fruit eaters.

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:16.950
Before becoming a bomb technician,

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:19.240
an interlocutor of mine served as a monk

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:21.620
for seven years at a
Theravada Buddhist temple

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:24.200
in Vientiane in the capital of Laos.

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:26.200
After his work day at the office

00:20:26.200 --> 00:20:28.310
of an explosives clearance operator

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:30.080
that is hosting my research,

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.700
he invites me to ride
with him on his motorbike

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:35.720
to visit his former temple Wat Sokpaulang

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:37.193
on the outskirts of town.

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:41.900
Wat Sokpaulang is a forest
temple originally situated

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:44.370
in the jungle outside the city walls,

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:45.830
though in recent decades,

00:20:45.830 --> 00:20:48.890
it has been encompassed by urban sprawl.

00:20:48.890 --> 00:20:52.440
The temple maintains
expansive forested grounds

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:56.160
circumnavigated by a white gilded wall.

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.280
Leaving our motorbike against
the wall we enter by foot

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:01.620
under a large lavishly painted archway

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:03.360
of entwined dragons.

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:05.950
As we cross into the forest and interior

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:07.950
the sound of the nearby thoroughfare

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:10.760
is smothered by vestigial forest preserved

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:13.340
within the temple grounds and underbrush

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:16.220
of flowering ginger and medicinal herbs.

00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:19.540
Plus large trees whose
trunks host pale lichens,

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:21.163
wax leaved bromeliads,

00:21:22.091 --> 00:21:24.610
ferns and trailing gray lianas.

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:28.070
When we arrive at the
central plaza next to the Sim

00:21:28.070 --> 00:21:30.370
the most sacred central building,

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:33.450
a ceremony of cremation is underway.

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:35.570
The relatives and guests have left

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:39.163
only two silent novices remain
to attend the giant kalm.

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:42.520
They kindle the fire with long sticks

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:44.230
producing snapping crackles

00:21:44.230 --> 00:21:46.410
that seem only to deepen the silence

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:47.910
in the plaza.

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:50.290
The air is diffused with blue smoke

00:21:50.290 --> 00:21:52.570
and the thick smell of incense.

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:55.740
The coffin has already collapsed
upon itself and the body

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:58.710
is no longer visible within the flames.

00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:02.410
Wreaths and other flowering
decorations smoke their green

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:05.670
moisture resisting the cremation.

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:07.410
We stay at the border of the plaza

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:10.230
and watch the flame
slowly diminish the corpse

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:11.323
and the flowers.

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:15.210
In this quiet zone of mortal reflection,

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:17.980
my companion tells me a strange,

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:21.010
brief war story he heard
while he was a monk

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:22.610
at this temple.

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:25.080
He told this story in the present tense

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:27.863
as he first heard it,
and as I tell it here.

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:32.210
Two American soldiers
are flying a helicopter

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:34.070
low over the jungles of Laos,

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:37.800
scouting for villages during
the Vietnam American war.

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:40.800
They are looking for
possible communist hideouts

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:42.920
sites where rebels built camps

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:45.710
or were hosted by existing villages.

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:49.420
Skimming the tree tops one
soldier sees very clearly

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:50.980
a village below.

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:53.170
Grass huts on stilts,

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:56.060
dirt worn paths through the green fields,

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:57.900
people walking with baskets of fruit

00:22:57.900 --> 00:22:59.750
strapped to their backs.

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:02.990
He signals a landing nearby but
once they are on the ground,

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:05.023
there is no village to be found.

00:23:05.870 --> 00:23:07.140
After giving up the search

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:08.870
and reboarding their helicopter,

00:23:08.870 --> 00:23:12.650
the two soldiers keep watch
out of the vehicle's windows.

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:14.160
Neither one sees a village,

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:17.363
the village and its
inhabitants disappeared.

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:21.070
In response to my perplexed expression,

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:23.380
my interlocutor explains,

00:23:23.380 --> 00:23:24.980
there are worlds around us

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:27.870
that we can't see full of people

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:31.280
who are right and honest
and only eat fruit.

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:32.673
They never kill anything.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:35.500
These worlds exist parallel

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:37.080
to the present realm,

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:39.570
other realms other heavens and hells.

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.970
My interlocutor spreads his arms wide

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:44.900
and sweeps them around
while wriggling his fingers

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:47.270
as if to touch the stuff
of these many worlds

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:49.033
all around us invisible.

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:52.850
The world that the soldiers
glimpsed in the war story

00:23:52.850 --> 00:23:55.800
is known as the realm of the fruit eaters.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:59.460
It is a special paradise
inhabited by merit field beings

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:02.120
who never kill anything
to sustain themselves.

00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:06.910
They only eat fruit that has
fallen freely from wild trees.

00:24:06.910 --> 00:24:09.310
This kind of food is free
from the negative karma

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:12.050
of slaughtering animals
or destroying plants.

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:14.350
It is a realm without butchers,

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:18.970
meat eaters, murderers,
executioners or soldiers.

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:20.760
As a result of the virtuous habits

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:21.600
of the residents,

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.860
the realm of the fruit eaters is peaceful

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:27.300
and all the beings who reside
there are healthy and happy.

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:30.110
Only especially morally correct beings

00:24:30.110 --> 00:24:33.040
are reincarnated as fruit eaters.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:36.250
By comparison, the immoral
actions of the inhabitants

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:38.720
of our world are creating a state

00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:42.960
of near perpetual war,
violence and suffering.

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:46.380
My interlocutor explains
that the Vietnam American War

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:49.450
made the boundaries between
these parallel worlds

00:24:49.450 --> 00:24:52.560
porous and so in the midst of war,

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:55.890
one American soldier
glimpses allow paradise

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:57.647
free of violence."

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:02.990
So that's the opening of my book,

00:25:02.990 --> 00:25:04.820
written in the present tense,

00:25:04.820 --> 00:25:07.410
in the same tense that
my companion told me

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:08.593
this war story.

00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:14.760
And later on, I was
reminded of the fruit eaters

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:18.120
while doing field work with
a bomb clearance squad.

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:20.220
When one of the technicians told me

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:23.010
that she remembered the
different kinds of bombs

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:25.910
because they all look like
different kinds of fruit.

00:25:25.910 --> 00:25:27.510
And she had fruit names for all

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:29.770
of the different kinds of bombs.

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:30.960
And in response to that,

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:35.190
I wrote a poem called "The Fruit Eaters,"

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:36.740
which I'll also share with you.

00:25:39.187 --> 00:25:42.250
"The exceedingly virtuous eat only fruit

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:45.093
that freely falls without
knowledge of death.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:50.850
She forges from the forest,
cucumber bombs, guava bombs,

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:54.803
bael bombs, pineapple bombs, melon bombs.

00:25:56.187 --> 00:25:58.350
"Sometimes I wonder if they are supposed

00:25:58.350 --> 00:25:59.370
to look like fruit

00:25:59.370 --> 00:26:00.977
so that we will pick them up."

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:04.740
She holds a yellow bomb
the size of her fist

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:07.647
with fins, like the blades of pineapples."

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:11.023
Thank you.

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:16.170
- Thank you, Leah.

00:26:16.170 --> 00:26:18.540
I hesitate to interrupt.

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:20.270
I want everyone to just pause

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:22.513
on the sound of that final poem.

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:33.020
Now let's take a moment
and turn over to Jonathan.

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:34.020
Jonathan, did you want me

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:35.430
to share your slides again?

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:37.460
I know you had a few more,

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:38.910
maybe for this scene.

00:26:38.910 --> 00:26:40.760
- You know we talked about this.

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:42.063
I think it's fine not to.

00:26:43.730 --> 00:26:45.460
I thought if people were sharing slides,

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:47.060
I'd share slides but no worries.

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:49.360
Okay, I'm gonna read just a section

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:51.320
from it's my turn yeah, Eric.

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:54.300
So sorry, I'll just read a section

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:56.487
from the book called "Forest of the Dead."

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:02.400
During this visit to
my friend Twic's farm,

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:05.040
just to note I referred to him as Jiang

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:09.630
as a form of address and
this reflects an alliance

00:27:11.350 --> 00:27:13.750
that we've consecrated
between the two of us so.

00:27:15.058 --> 00:27:16.480
"I went to visit Kasoa Twic

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:19.300
at his farm on the Yabla
to the east of the village

00:27:19.300 --> 00:27:21.040
where the mountains of Vietnam loom off

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:22.380
in the distance.

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:24.350
Twic and his neighbors
have cut their fields

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:26.450
from a broad expansive forest.

00:27:26.450 --> 00:27:28.610
Here the farmland slopes gradually up

00:27:28.610 --> 00:27:31.270
from the stream, and
ends in a fringe of trees

00:27:31.270 --> 00:27:33.620
and scrub at the top of the ridge.

00:27:33.620 --> 00:27:35.760
Between the stream and the forests edge

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:38.040
all has been cleared and planted,

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:39.820
low lying cashews at the bottom

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:42.140
of the hill, sesame, tobacco,

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:45.140
maize and rice in the more
recently created fields

00:27:45.140 --> 00:27:46.550
towards the top.

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:50.930
All has been cleared that is,
except for a shock of trees

00:27:50.930 --> 00:27:54.060
that jets out in the midst
of Twic's farm field,

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.960
a 400 square foot stand
of forest in the middle

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:58.463
of acres of farmland.

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:02.810
The day I arrived, I found
Twic working the edges

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:05.370
of that anomalous cluster of trees.

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:07.510
He stood shirtless and covered in sweat,

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:09.240
a bush knife in his hand,

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:10.840
his torso streaked black

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:13.490
where the charred branches
he carried had marked him.

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:16.720
He was clearing brush from
the edge of the corpse

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:18.340
removing it to small piles

00:28:18.340 --> 00:28:19.840
that he had set alight,

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:22.590
and as those piles burned all around,

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:26.850
he assessed the corpses
edge inside, exasperated,

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:30.080
or perhaps it was a kind
of mock exasperation.

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.450
This was common with
Twic that he would laugh

00:28:32.450 --> 00:28:33.690
about his predicament.

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:34.750
Not with irony,

00:28:34.750 --> 00:28:36.900
but with complete sincerity.

00:28:36.900 --> 00:28:39.810
'Oh, Jiang," I addressed
him as I walked up.

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:42.047
He smiled, happy to see me.

00:28:42.047 --> 00:28:44.460
"You look frustrated, what's the matter?"

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:45.750
He sighed again,

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:48.280
still smiling as he
looked from burning piles

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:50.097
to the shaggy forest.

00:28:50.097 --> 00:28:52.247
"The dead", he said.

00:28:52.247 --> 00:28:53.757
"It's the dead Jiang."

00:28:54.930 --> 00:28:57.360
It's not just the spirits known as Yang,

00:28:57.360 --> 00:28:59.610
who inhabit the forests
of the Northeast hills

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:03.090
but also a bow the dead who live there.

00:29:03.090 --> 00:29:05.210
Villages produce dead people,

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:06.610
and the living bury the dead

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:09.080
in the forest at the villages edge.

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:11.970
There upon those forests
may no longer be cut,

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:14.410
they must be left to grow freely.

00:29:14.410 --> 00:29:16.810
This is why one finds funerary statues

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:18.710
under the tallest patches of forest

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:21.500
in areas where villages once stood.

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:23.340
When a village site is abandoned,

00:29:23.340 --> 00:29:26.520
the village burial ground
becomes its own forest type,

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:30.150
a forest of the dead, a glide and fowl.

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:32.940
This is an ecological
formation and a social world

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:34.140
at the same time,

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:36.520
a forest animated not just by memory,

00:29:36.520 --> 00:29:38.323
but by human presences too.

00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:42.240
It was just such a socio
ecological formation that Twic

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:43.920
was dealing with here in the middle

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:46.720
of his agricultural lands
where the forest of the dead

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:49.140
was shading out his
crops and taking up space

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:51.880
he might otherwise use for farming.

00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:53.980
Twic he told me there
were jars and statues

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:56.950
and tombs inside the
darkness under those trees.

00:29:56.950 --> 00:29:59.130
He invited me to enter the corpse

00:29:59.130 --> 00:30:02.050
and poke around no harm would come to me.

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:05.920
So I did just passed the
tangled edge where binds

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:07.720
and shrubbery crowded the light,

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:09.810
I found a decaying statue that resembled

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:13.310
the antenna of an insect
or perhaps elephant tusks.

00:30:13.310 --> 00:30:17.550
A familiar funerary form, and
large shreds of ceramic jars

00:30:17.550 --> 00:30:20.190
emerging from the ground where
they had once been placed

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:21.690
at the head of a tomb.

00:30:21.690 --> 00:30:24.910
It was a graveyard, but who's?"

00:30:24.910 --> 00:30:27.790
So the intervening couple of pages Twic

00:30:27.790 --> 00:30:31.730
and his wife, Mon, tell me the story

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:33.310
of whose graves those are.

00:30:33.310 --> 00:30:35.870
And this is the story of this village

00:30:35.870 --> 00:30:39.220
from Pleiku province in Vietnam,

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:42.350
which had come across
the river to Cambodia

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:46.270
to flee the rolling
thunder bombing campaign

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:50.843
and this series of bombings
that was taking place

00:30:55.670 --> 00:30:58.770
in just across the river at that time.

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:02.640
And so what happens is that this village

00:31:02.640 --> 00:31:07.020
actually seeks shelter with
the villagers of Tankahuan,

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:10.110
who they have some relations
with some kin relations.

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:12.510
And they're granted this place to come

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:14.270
and do their farming.

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:15.300
But at this moment,

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:19.310
the American bombing
campaign begins in Cambodia,

00:31:19.310 --> 00:31:23.710
and a bomb is dropped on the settlement

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:25.963
killing everybody in the settlement.

00:31:27.830 --> 00:31:32.440
And then, some of the
relatives came across

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:36.680
and the survivors, what
people were able to find

00:31:36.680 --> 00:31:38.340
were buried in the site.

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:41.790
And I just wanted to finish up here

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:45.170
with a couple more lines
of where this winds up

00:31:45.170 --> 00:31:46.460
because I think you can see a little bit

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:49.367
of how the history is worked
into the lived experience

00:31:49.367 --> 00:31:51.127
of the landscape.

00:31:51.127 --> 00:31:53.640
"The scene I encountered
in Twic's field illustrates

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:58.000
how the forest as forests and
as socio ecological formation

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:00.070
acts upon the world.

00:32:00.070 --> 00:32:03.020
In his appraisal of the
edge of the Galayatal

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:05.960
in his swidden field Twic
used his capabilities

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:09.310
as a farmer and as a
Jarai person to consider

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:12.490
how he should treat the
small cluster of trees.

00:32:12.490 --> 00:32:14.510
It would have been more
efficient from the perspective

00:32:14.510 --> 00:32:17.650
of agricultural productivity
to do away completely

00:32:17.650 --> 00:32:20.470
with the tangled patch
of forest and graves.

00:32:20.470 --> 00:32:23.650
But Twic did not conceive of
the situation in these terms.

00:32:23.650 --> 00:32:26.580
Rather, he faced a dilemma
of what should be burned

00:32:26.580 --> 00:32:29.400
and what should be left and
where the edge should be,

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:31.680
and how thoroughly he
should intervene to balance

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:33.950
a set of objectives and requirements

00:32:33.950 --> 00:32:35.710
as he understood them.

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:38.670
There were aesthetic as well
as moral questions involved,

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:40.793
the landscape should look a certain way.

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:44.320
It should include certain
plants arranged appropriately

00:32:44.320 --> 00:32:46.560
in relation to each other.

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:48.160
The corpse was replete with memory

00:32:48.160 --> 00:32:51.130
and meaning it had a
representational force

00:32:51.130 --> 00:32:52.900
that acted upon Twic.

00:32:52.900 --> 00:32:55.000
But it was also animated by the presence

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:57.830
of vietaw, the dead who
resided in the forest

00:32:57.830 --> 00:33:00.120
and who required respect.

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:02.360
Finally, it was also very much alive,

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:04.810
it was growing as it had
been since the victims

00:33:04.810 --> 00:33:06.690
of the American bomb had been buried

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:08.210
in the spot.

00:33:08.210 --> 00:33:10.500
Likewise, of course Twic's swidden field

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:13.230
was itself alive both
with crops he had planted

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:14.690
and with incidental species

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:17.030
that were either desired or not desired,

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:19.050
based on their usefulness.

00:33:19.050 --> 00:33:20.830
And all the plants themselves furthermore,

00:33:20.830 --> 00:33:24.160
possessed pungat what we might call souls,

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:25.470
and thus, at the very least,

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:28.460
must be understood to
have a certain vitality.

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:31.400
That is, in a way comparable
with that of humans,

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:34.170
who also, of course, also have pungat.

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:36.530
The multiple vital
presences with which Twic

00:33:36.530 --> 00:33:39.080
was necessarily required to negotiate

00:33:39.080 --> 00:33:41.690
make up the landscape,
not so much a productivity

00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:44.560
problem to be solved as
a set of relationships

00:33:44.560 --> 00:33:46.420
to be managed and attended to."

00:33:46.420 --> 00:33:47.693
So it goes on from there.

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:52.893
- Thank you, Jonathan.

00:33:53.770 --> 00:33:57.313
It's amazing to hear ethnography read.

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:00.760
It's something we often
don't give ourselves

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:01.900
the opportunity to do.

00:34:01.900 --> 00:34:05.250
And that's quite powerful
ethnography written there.

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:06.840
Let me turn it over to David Biggs

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:08.790
for the last section of this.

00:34:08.790 --> 00:34:09.870
And then after that,

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:11.000
I just wanna encourage people

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:12.700
to start formulating questions,

00:34:12.700 --> 00:34:13.640
cause we'll open it up

00:34:13.640 --> 00:34:15.930
to the audience for questions after that.

00:34:15.930 --> 00:34:16.763
Go ahead, David.

00:34:16.763 --> 00:34:17.596
- Thanks, Eric.

00:34:17.596 --> 00:34:19.530
And, for mine if you can share the screen,

00:34:19.530 --> 00:34:22.603
I've got a slide the third slide there.

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:25.420
It'd be good.

00:34:25.420 --> 00:34:28.120
It'll help people understand where we are.

00:34:28.120 --> 00:34:30.303
So I'm gonna read a passage.

00:34:33.825 --> 00:34:36.500
I don't know, it's not as ethnographic.

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:37.850
So I hope you'll bear with me,

00:34:37.850 --> 00:34:39.460
but it's a very important story

00:34:40.910 --> 00:34:43.163
in the sense for, one before that.

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:48.510
So it's a very important
story for really reading

00:34:49.570 --> 00:34:52.210
very famous military events.

00:34:52.210 --> 00:34:55.880
And I think everybody on
the call knows that in 1968,

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:58.960
the Tet Offensive, the
Battle of Hue was this sort

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:01.930
of surprise attack on the city of Hue.

00:35:01.930 --> 00:35:04.780
It's often retold in
so many different ways,

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:06.310
so many different movies,

00:35:06.310 --> 00:35:08.650
I think, full metal jacket and all

00:35:08.650 --> 00:35:11.100
these other Hollywood stories.

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:13.360
All Americans know the story.

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:16.940
And what's so amazing to
me is that they don't know

00:35:16.940 --> 00:35:19.880
that this wasn't the first
Tet Offensive in Hue.

00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:23.080
In fact, this was the
second or even the third

00:35:23.080 --> 00:35:27.250
in a sense, and that French
troops land on these beaches

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:31.390
in 1883 but that's in the summer.

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:32.900
But I'm gonna tell you a story,

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:35.450
because I think it's very
important when we talk

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:40.190
about militarization to work
against presentist bias.

00:35:40.190 --> 00:35:42.160
And especially writing in English

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:44.920
most Anglophone readers are very familiar

00:35:44.920 --> 00:35:46.540
with the most recent war,

00:35:46.540 --> 00:35:48.380
but we have a tendency to forget

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:50.490
about the earlier layers.

00:35:50.490 --> 00:35:52.920
And again, if we talk about the agency

00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:54.950
of militarized landscapes,

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:58.080
in a sense militarization
as a set of processes

00:35:58.080 --> 00:36:01.500
from one era bleeds into subsequent eras.

00:36:01.500 --> 00:36:04.130
So I wanna read this section from my book

00:36:04.130 --> 00:36:05.520
in chapter three,

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:08.640
about the first Tet Offensive.

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:10.180
And I wanna read it in part

00:36:10.180 --> 00:36:13.460
so you might understand
why it was, how it was

00:36:13.460 --> 00:36:16.421
that two battalions of soldiers were able

00:36:16.421 --> 00:36:21.421
to secretly put themselves in
the city and rise up in 1968.

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:26.110
And I think when you have
this first Tet Offensive,

00:36:26.110 --> 00:36:29.787
it might give you some context, right.

00:36:29.787 --> 00:36:33.980
"So fighting between Viet
Minh and French forces began

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:37.696
on December 19 1946, in Hue Fau.

00:36:37.696 --> 00:36:38.529
And a destructive French naval

00:36:38.529 --> 00:36:40.950
invasion at Hue commenced a month later

00:36:40.950 --> 00:36:43.440
sparing thousands of
young people to retreat

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:46.240
from lowland defences
to the tactical zones.

00:36:46.240 --> 00:36:48.120
In the months leading up to the invasion,

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:50.950
the Viet Minh urged its
followers to dig tunnels,

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:53.000
trenches and barricades.

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:54.940
In the city of Hue in nearby villages,

00:36:54.940 --> 00:36:57.730
several thousand youth
dug defensive positions,

00:36:57.730 --> 00:36:59.960
with bomb shelters and tunnels.

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:02.800
Upon receiving word about
the fighting at Hue Fang

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:05.260
Vietnam units in Hue blew all the bridges

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:06.890
along Highway One.

00:37:06.890 --> 00:37:10.280
The admin troops in Hue
attacked about 800 outnumbered

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:13.380
but well armed French
troops at the marine hotel,

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:14.880
and other government buildings

00:37:14.880 --> 00:37:16.750
in the French quarter.

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:19.260
Unlike the naval assault in 1883,

00:37:19.260 --> 00:37:21.890
this military landing
met fierce resistance

00:37:21.890 --> 00:37:24.870
from the landing beaches
to the streets of Hue.

00:37:24.870 --> 00:37:26.840
Once just five days before the start

00:37:26.840 --> 00:37:28.060
of the Lunar New Year,

00:37:28.060 --> 00:37:31.780
this 1947 Tet Offensive
resulted in high numbers

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:34.080
of civilian casualties and left sections

00:37:34.080 --> 00:37:35.660
of the town in ruins.

00:37:35.660 --> 00:37:38.600
French Marines, most of
them from French Africa,

00:37:38.600 --> 00:37:40.710
brought naval artillery and heavy weapons

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:43.770
against lightly armed but again Viet Minh.

00:37:43.770 --> 00:37:46.780
Viet Minh forces had
governed Hue for over a year

00:37:46.780 --> 00:37:49.670
after the August revolution
and even after the return

00:37:49.670 --> 00:37:52.850
of French soldiers they
commanded a military regiment

00:37:52.850 --> 00:37:55.500
and enjoyed strong
support in the villages.

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:58.530
Their units engaged French
forces invading by sea

00:37:58.530 --> 00:38:01.313
at two inlands Tu Hien and Thuon An.

00:38:02.880 --> 00:38:05.970
French forces landed on the
beach at SantoKai casualties.

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:08.800
One group a few thousand
troops with armored cars

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:12.220
loaded on American made
military landing craft surplus

00:38:12.220 --> 00:38:14.960
after world war two unloaded at the banks

00:38:14.960 --> 00:38:17.670
of the lagoon at Truoi
within a few hundred meters

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:19.170
of Highway One.

00:38:19.170 --> 00:38:20.980
The other group landed at Tu Hien beach

00:38:20.980 --> 00:38:24.110
with armored vehicles to
attack Hue from the north.

00:38:24.110 --> 00:38:27.490
In contrast to the French
naval landing in 1883,

00:38:27.490 --> 00:38:30.530
the forces in 1947 met
sustained resistance

00:38:30.530 --> 00:38:32.550
from the beach to the city.

00:38:32.550 --> 00:38:36.200
The southern group required 19
days to travel 30 kilometers

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:38.720
on Highway One from Truoi before meeting

00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:39.953
the other group in Hue.

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:44.240
From Hue and Phu Bai French
forces spent the next month

00:38:44.240 --> 00:38:46.960
reoccupying key military installations

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:49.900
finding them emptied with
all supplies removed.

00:38:49.900 --> 00:38:53.040
As the French troops secured
positions along Highway one,

00:38:53.040 --> 00:38:55.350
they destroyed village communal houses

00:38:55.350 --> 00:38:57.290
at Da Le and Phu Bai

00:38:57.290 --> 00:38:58.670
and they set up command posts

00:38:58.670 --> 00:39:01.720
at some of the region's most sacred sites.

00:39:01.720 --> 00:39:04.260
One of them was Nam Dong pavilion

00:39:04.260 --> 00:39:06.390
on the southern edge of Hue.

00:39:06.390 --> 00:39:08.880
Nam Dong included pine
gross and a pavilion

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:11.930
that hosted the annual
rights the Emperor performed

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:13.670
to bring prosperity and good harvest

00:39:13.670 --> 00:39:14.770
to the kingdom.

00:39:14.770 --> 00:39:17.120
The profaning of the
site with French troops

00:39:17.120 --> 00:39:18.510
and military equipment,

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:21.430
especially bombs gave a clear visual sign

00:39:21.430 --> 00:39:23.760
of the French position
vis-a- vis the recently

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:26.300
advocated head of state Valdai.

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:27.820
On the edge of the French Quarter

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:30.550
in the deforested spaces of Nan Zhau

00:39:30.550 --> 00:39:34.100
the French military established
its Nodanam Hue sector."

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:37.140
So this is an example of a symbolic attack

00:39:37.140 --> 00:39:39.207
on this landscape.

00:39:39.207 --> 00:39:41.810
"After French forces broke
the Viet Minh defense

00:39:41.810 --> 00:39:44.690
Viet Minh units retreated
to tactical zones.

00:39:44.690 --> 00:39:47.270
Over the next several
years this move up hill

00:39:47.270 --> 00:39:50.850
Zoo to facilitated profound territorial

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:52.920
and personal transformations,

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:55.890
especially for thousands
of Vietnamese youth.

00:39:55.890 --> 00:39:57.490
The political and military leaders

00:39:57.490 --> 00:39:58.900
of the Viet Minh had regroup

00:39:58.900 --> 00:40:01.130
with their families at Hoa Mi for more

00:40:01.130 --> 00:40:03.757
than a year since 1946.

00:40:03.757 --> 00:40:07.370
The Drun Gavan regimens, three
battalions retreated here

00:40:07.370 --> 00:40:09.340
and formed a company of commandos

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:12.403
to continue guerrilla
missions into the city.

00:40:13.410 --> 00:40:16.870
After the French invasion
in the summer of 1947,

00:40:16.870 --> 00:40:19.610
more army units and larger
communities so soldiers

00:40:19.610 --> 00:40:22.050
and their families continued the Exodus,

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:24.260
expanding tunnel networks
into the mountains

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:26.970
and digging bunkers
and underground caches.

00:40:26.970 --> 00:40:29.010
The province committee anticipated

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:31.530
that French occupation
forces would force youths

00:40:31.530 --> 00:40:32.600
in the lowlands

00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:34.790
to serve with the occupation military.

00:40:34.790 --> 00:40:36.830
So it built tactical zones

00:40:36.830 --> 00:40:38.740
as spaces for removing young men

00:40:38.740 --> 00:40:41.020
from their mid teens to late 30s

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:43.100
from villages along Highway one.

00:40:43.100 --> 00:40:45.020
They established routes of communication,

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:46.700
trails and secret codes

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:48.150
to guide youth volunteers

00:40:48.150 --> 00:40:49.460
into the war zones,

00:40:49.460 --> 00:40:51.250
opening paths for escape,

00:40:51.250 --> 00:40:53.530
and for guerrilla teams to return."

00:40:53.530 --> 00:40:56.250
And so those red dots you
see those are the first four

00:40:56.250 --> 00:41:00.567
of those tactical zones that
were created on those rivers.

00:41:00.567 --> 00:41:03.240
"While most histories focus
on the tactical elements

00:41:03.240 --> 00:41:06.740
of these zones, a
hometwee district account

00:41:06.740 --> 00:41:09.410
of the resistance capture
some glimpses of the tolls

00:41:09.410 --> 00:41:11.750
on families in occupied villages

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:14.550
whose sons and daughters had gone up hill.

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:17.280
Resistant zones were
difficult places to survive

00:41:17.280 --> 00:41:19.310
even without enemy attacks.

00:41:19.310 --> 00:41:22.240
Mosquitoes carried malaria
and Viet Minh field hospitals

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:26.120
were lucky to have quinine
as a preventative medicine.

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:28.410
Young women with limited
training as nurses

00:41:28.410 --> 00:41:31.630
regularly risked search
detention and torture

00:41:31.630 --> 00:41:34.050
for carrying medicine
from towns such as Hue

00:41:34.050 --> 00:41:35.960
into these rebels areas.

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:39.140
One doctor left a
prosperous practice in Hue

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:40.610
to carry medical supplies

00:41:40.610 --> 00:41:44.120
to the field hospital
at the Khe Trai base.

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:47.130
The tactical zones foster
deep emotional connections

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:49.440
linking people from
different lowland villages

00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:50.700
with each camp.

00:41:50.700 --> 00:41:53.961
A district history describes
this yearning for relatives,

00:41:53.961 --> 00:41:56.890
"the tactical zone was
a place of much longing,

00:41:56.890 --> 00:41:59.970
excitement for reunions
and deep compassion.

00:41:59.970 --> 00:42:01.610
Here was the basis for understanding

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:03.860
the preciousness of life.

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:06.380
In the afternoon, the people of Fu Vong

00:42:06.380 --> 00:42:08.970
and Hue Twi districts
look from their homes

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:12.400
in the countryside to the
tactical zones in the hills,

00:42:12.400 --> 00:42:13.930
remembering those working there

00:42:13.930 --> 00:42:17.280
in service, transport,
communication, logistics,

00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:18.890
remembering their first trips

00:42:18.890 --> 00:42:20.617
into the tactical zones."

00:42:21.720 --> 00:42:23.950
As the resistance
continued for seven years,

00:42:23.950 --> 00:42:26.620
these emotional ties in
the traffic in and out

00:42:26.620 --> 00:42:29.370
of the region, help
forged new trails and webs

00:42:29.370 --> 00:42:30.680
of local networks,

00:42:30.680 --> 00:42:32.640
through which the Viet
Minh national vision

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:37.050
extended across more of the
central coast, rugged terrain.

00:42:37.050 --> 00:42:38.340
And now, Eric, if you can go

00:42:38.340 --> 00:42:39.630
to the next slide.

00:42:39.630 --> 00:42:40.463
Real quick.

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:45.253
Yeah.

00:42:45.253 --> 00:42:48.730
So this is a close up
of that place Hoa My.

00:42:48.730 --> 00:42:51.550
And the point I'm trying to make here

00:42:51.550 --> 00:42:54.260
is that these earlier layer

00:42:54.260 --> 00:42:59.220
of military conflict,
it wasn't just tactical

00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:00.420
and moving on the land,

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:03.670
but it actually created
new social relationships.

00:43:03.670 --> 00:43:05.630
And it created new interactions,

00:43:05.630 --> 00:43:09.370
new symbolic and connections
with this landscape.

00:43:09.370 --> 00:43:11.800
You see in the upper
right corner of this map

00:43:11.800 --> 00:43:14.600
on the right, that's
highway one and the railroad

00:43:14.600 --> 00:43:16.180
just north of Hue.

00:43:16.180 --> 00:43:17.710
That's the street Wie Troi

00:43:17.710 --> 00:43:19.840
that Bernard Fall writes about.

00:43:19.840 --> 00:43:22.520
And this is a place called Hoa My.

00:43:22.520 --> 00:43:23.710
And what's so interesting,

00:43:23.710 --> 00:43:25.760
if you read this map closely,

00:43:25.760 --> 00:43:28.040
you can see the names of the hilltops

00:43:28.040 --> 00:43:28.873
to the right.

00:43:28.873 --> 00:43:31.440
And the hilltops to the
right have all been cleared.

00:43:31.440 --> 00:43:34.263
They have an in, the letter in means Nui.

00:43:35.270 --> 00:43:37.700
And if you look to the left, however,

00:43:37.700 --> 00:43:39.410
this is another French map.

00:43:39.410 --> 00:43:41.570
This is a topographic map,

00:43:41.570 --> 00:43:44.910
drawn and then reproduced in 1952

00:43:44.910 --> 00:43:48.850
by the US Army mapping
service for the French.

00:43:48.850 --> 00:43:50.630
You see the UTM grid,

00:43:50.630 --> 00:43:53.150
this purple grid that's overlaid.

00:43:53.150 --> 00:43:56.120
That's a world war two
military grid system

00:43:56.120 --> 00:43:59.050
the Americans use and the allies use

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:00.540
and that was used for calling

00:44:00.540 --> 00:44:03.100
in bombing coordinates.

00:44:03.100 --> 00:44:05.100
But what's really interesting

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:07.210
are the ridge names on the left.

00:44:07.210 --> 00:44:09.870
You see a transition from the word Nui

00:44:09.870 --> 00:44:11.300
to a word Gulp,

00:44:11.300 --> 00:44:15.330
and a word dumb and gulm signify,

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:18.230
not ethnically Vietnamese hilltops.

00:44:18.230 --> 00:44:21.060
And so this gateway,

00:44:21.060 --> 00:44:25.160
that is an important tactical
zone for the Viet Minh youth

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:28.220
is also a really important
cultural gateway.

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:30.630
It's the place in which
so many of these youth

00:44:30.630 --> 00:44:34.640
entered into these trails
that then move further west

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:37.360
and north up to the
battle zones in the north

00:44:37.360 --> 00:44:40.720
of Vietnam during the
French Indochina War.

00:44:40.720 --> 00:44:43.430
And so there's a very important kind

00:44:43.430 --> 00:44:48.020
of spatial practice that
is happening in 1947

00:44:48.020 --> 00:44:49.060
for these youth.

00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:52.090
Many of them get relocated after 1954

00:44:52.090 --> 00:44:54.720
as combatants to Tanwar.

00:44:54.720 --> 00:44:58.190
They become part of some
Hue area central Vietnamese

00:44:58.190 --> 00:45:01.500
regiments in the People's Army of Vietnam.

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:05.320
And again, what's
interesting is that by 1966,

00:45:05.320 --> 00:45:09.470
some of these regimental
units are back in this area.

00:45:09.470 --> 00:45:11.430
And so one of the points I try and make

00:45:11.430 --> 00:45:16.430
in my book is that if
we take a deeper study

00:45:16.820 --> 00:45:21.110
of militarization in these
particularly militarized spaces,

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:24.530
we can better understand
how different groups

00:45:24.530 --> 00:45:26.860
of combatants move through these spaces

00:45:26.860 --> 00:45:28.210
and how they understand them.

00:45:28.210 --> 00:45:31.090
And I think that applies
not just to Vietnam,

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:32.910
it applies to Afghanistan,

00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:37.140
to Iraq and to desert areas

00:45:37.140 --> 00:45:38.640
and other parts of the world.

00:45:38.640 --> 00:45:41.220
And I think it's that presenters bias

00:45:41.220 --> 00:45:42.653
that is really missing.

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:46.670
I do have one last story to share.

00:45:46.670 --> 00:45:48.463
But I'm not sure if I have time.

00:45:49.470 --> 00:45:50.303
Eric.

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:54.580
- David, you can share your story.

00:45:54.580 --> 00:45:56.450
- Okay, thanks.

00:45:56.450 --> 00:45:58.730
It's towards the end of the book.

00:45:58.730 --> 00:46:01.070
And it's this final
kind of epilogue chapter

00:46:01.070 --> 00:46:02.773
if you can go to the next slide.

00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:06.680
Oh, and I'll say one more thing

00:46:06.680 --> 00:46:08.170
about this place Hoa My.

00:46:08.170 --> 00:46:12.170
So Hoa My was so symbolically
important for the Viet Minh.

00:46:12.170 --> 00:46:14.790
It was also very symbolically important

00:46:14.790 --> 00:46:18.370
for the Hue family, the mode Enzian family

00:46:18.370 --> 00:46:20.040
and his family.

00:46:20.040 --> 00:46:22.720
That when they got American
aid money to develop

00:46:22.720 --> 00:46:25.600
these new strategic Hamlet's
and things like that,

00:46:25.600 --> 00:46:26.780
the first place they picked to send

00:46:26.780 --> 00:46:29.413
the bulldozers was Hoa My.

00:46:30.730 --> 00:46:32.530
They wanted to relocate people there

00:46:32.530 --> 00:46:35.040
not because it was a great
place for agriculture,

00:46:35.040 --> 00:46:38.630
but because symbolically
it was a way to level

00:46:38.630 --> 00:46:41.710
and erase the important foothold

00:46:41.710 --> 00:46:44.150
that the Viet Minh had
established in this place.

00:46:44.150 --> 00:46:46.080
So Americans had no clue.

00:46:46.080 --> 00:46:49.100
But these local actors
knew exactly symbolically

00:46:49.100 --> 00:46:50.530
why they were doing that.

00:46:50.530 --> 00:46:52.780
Okay, so this is the last
one and this gets back

00:46:52.780 --> 00:46:54.940
to this base footprint,

00:46:54.940 --> 00:46:56.880
this former military base,

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:59.820
which now is a very large industrial area,

00:46:59.820 --> 00:47:01.013
industrial park.

00:47:02.680 --> 00:47:07.200
And this centers on an
interview that I did

00:47:07.200 --> 00:47:10.170
with former village head
on the village bordering

00:47:10.170 --> 00:47:11.003
this space.

00:47:11.003 --> 00:47:12.020
Some of the village lands,

00:47:12.020 --> 00:47:14.500
especially the village cemetery,

00:47:14.500 --> 00:47:18.110
was taken for Camp Eagle and
you actually have pictures

00:47:18.110 --> 00:47:21.090
of U.S troops and their barracks situated

00:47:21.090 --> 00:47:23.310
among Vietnamese tombs.

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:25.560
And the marines when they first used this

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:27.590
area called the Elzey tombstone,

00:47:27.590 --> 00:47:30.900
because the landing zone
was set amid the tombstones.

00:47:30.900 --> 00:47:34.850
Can you imagine what an
awful place that was,

00:47:34.850 --> 00:47:35.770
to fight a war?

00:47:35.770 --> 00:47:39.160
Anyway, this is about
what to do after the war.

00:47:39.160 --> 00:47:41.010
And this is a discussion with this man,

00:47:41.010 --> 00:47:42.823
Mr. Fog, and he said,

00:47:45.597 --> 00:47:48.240
"the decision about what
vestiges to keep or let go

00:47:48.240 --> 00:47:51.177
is complex when with many
parallels around the world."

00:47:51.177 --> 00:47:53.320
"Landscapes are often
at the center of debates

00:47:53.320 --> 00:47:55.560
involving veterans national history,

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:59.150
walls pockmarked by shrapnel
or metal skeletons of tanks

00:47:59.150 --> 00:48:01.930
and down planes often
serve as focal points

00:48:01.930 --> 00:48:04.410
for state guided reflection.

00:48:04.410 --> 00:48:08.040
Proponents of memorialization
seek such ruin monuments

00:48:08.040 --> 00:48:10.170
so that people will never forget.

00:48:10.170 --> 00:48:13.150
While others seek to erase
these old word vestiges

00:48:13.150 --> 00:48:14.700
to move on.

00:48:14.700 --> 00:48:17.210
Communities are often
divided too in trying

00:48:17.210 --> 00:48:18.960
to balance the desires of veterans

00:48:18.960 --> 00:48:20.490
in the younger generation.

00:48:20.490 --> 00:48:23.190
Mr. Fuller, the resident of Yatlay village

00:48:23.190 --> 00:48:26.170
who fought with the 324th regiment.

00:48:26.170 --> 00:48:28.360
That's one of those central
Vietnamese regiments

00:48:28.360 --> 00:48:32.550
that moved north described
to me as shock after 1975

00:48:32.550 --> 00:48:35.268
when the one time cityscape of Camp Eagle

00:48:35.268 --> 00:48:37.180
"looking like New York.

00:48:37.180 --> 00:48:38.880
Bright lights like crazy,

00:48:38.880 --> 00:48:41.030
turned into an empty wasteland"

00:48:41.030 --> 00:48:42.580
that you see here.

00:48:42.580 --> 00:48:44.910
A native of the village
and a political officer,

00:48:44.910 --> 00:48:47.300
he returned to the village
and hoped to preserve

00:48:47.300 --> 00:48:49.780
parts of Camp Eagle as a museum.

00:48:49.780 --> 00:48:52.330
While also salvaging
some of the metal siding

00:48:52.330 --> 00:48:53.890
to build his house.

00:48:53.890 --> 00:48:56.380
He went north to Hanoi for training.

00:48:56.380 --> 00:48:58.220
And when he returned,

00:48:58.220 --> 00:49:00.170
the tanks were all gone."

00:49:00.170 --> 00:49:02.230
He said, "they cleared the hills

00:49:02.230 --> 00:49:03.520
so that several years later,

00:49:03.520 --> 00:49:05.060
there was nothing left.

00:49:05.060 --> 00:49:07.380
Before when I looked from this place

00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:08.930
out it was New York City,

00:49:08.930 --> 00:49:10.470
but then they cleared it

00:49:10.470 --> 00:49:11.540
and left it empty.

00:49:11.540 --> 00:49:13.400
I returned in just farm my house

00:49:13.400 --> 00:49:16.170
was an iron sighting house just built.

00:49:16.170 --> 00:49:19.250
At that time, I resolved to
preserve one military tank

00:49:19.250 --> 00:49:20.365
as a reminder" and you see this

00:49:20.365 --> 00:49:22.280
in some Vietnamese villages,

00:49:22.280 --> 00:49:24.017
the lone military tank.

00:49:24.017 --> 00:49:25.700
"But then I went to Hanoi to study

00:49:25.700 --> 00:49:27.780
and the people here they took it for scrap

00:49:27.780 --> 00:49:29.170
removing everything.

00:49:29.170 --> 00:49:31.520
I had intended to
preserve the American base

00:49:31.520 --> 00:49:33.660
in Hamlet five, because I live near it.

00:49:33.660 --> 00:49:36.120
I knew to preserve the base intact,

00:49:36.120 --> 00:49:38.720
so that later our children
and people could come

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:41.430
and visit but they had taken
and had broken up everything.

00:49:41.430 --> 00:49:42.860
The stuff the Americans left

00:49:42.860 --> 00:49:44.097
was all destroyed."

00:49:44.960 --> 00:49:49.960
And so this continues this
debate in Vietnamese society

00:49:50.170 --> 00:49:52.760
about what to preserve and
how to preserve and of course,

00:49:52.760 --> 00:49:54.100
it's very political.

00:49:54.100 --> 00:49:56.030
And it's not only Vietnamese, of course,

00:49:56.030 --> 00:49:59.573
Americans have very strong
feelings about these places too.

00:50:00.720 --> 00:50:04.490
And for example one
American reporter traveled

00:50:04.490 --> 00:50:06.560
to this site about a year after this film

00:50:06.560 --> 00:50:08.120
was shot a guy named Craig Whitney

00:50:08.120 --> 00:50:09.400
with the New York Times.

00:50:09.400 --> 00:50:11.410
And in course he reports all that's left

00:50:11.410 --> 00:50:15.130
is rubble and a few stones
and an American veteran

00:50:15.130 --> 00:50:17.400
of the a hundred and first
airborne writes in the letter

00:50:17.400 --> 00:50:19.777
to the editor, he says,

00:50:19.777 --> 00:50:22.580
"I must respectfully disagree
with this observation

00:50:22.580 --> 00:50:26.210
that only stones were left
where American basis had stood.

00:50:26.210 --> 00:50:28.110
As a former noncommissioned officer

00:50:28.110 --> 00:50:31.090
in the hundred and first who
spent a short but incredibly

00:50:31.090 --> 00:50:34.130
intense part of my life
at Phu Bai in camp Eagle,

00:50:34.130 --> 00:50:36.050
I know with certainty
that more was left there,

00:50:36.050 --> 00:50:37.460
then a few stones.

00:50:37.460 --> 00:50:39.780
Things like honor and comradeship

00:50:39.780 --> 00:50:41.270
are former naivete,

00:50:41.270 --> 00:50:44.500
and the blood of ourselves
and others in the sand.

00:50:44.500 --> 00:50:46.207
For those of us who fought there Phu Bai

00:50:46.207 --> 00:50:49.530
and Camp Eagle remains
something indelible.

00:50:49.530 --> 00:50:51.910
A place in our minds and memories,

00:50:51.910 --> 00:50:53.607
not just places on a map."

00:50:54.660 --> 00:50:55.900
And so I leave you with that.

00:50:55.900 --> 00:50:57.277
But I think it's important.

00:50:57.277 --> 00:51:00.883
And I'm really interested
in our conversation today,

00:51:02.030 --> 00:51:03.820
to think about militarization,

00:51:03.820 --> 00:51:06.070
not simply in strict physical

00:51:06.070 --> 00:51:07.820
or presentist terms,

00:51:07.820 --> 00:51:11.453
but in these sort of
deeper layers and meanings.

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:15.150
- Thank you, David.

00:51:15.150 --> 00:51:18.910
It's actually quite amazing how a turn

00:51:18.910 --> 00:51:20.630
towards the landscape,

00:51:20.630 --> 00:51:23.060
which in some ways seems
to be the most material

00:51:23.060 --> 00:51:24.480
of all things,

00:51:24.480 --> 00:51:27.270
actually brings you back to the immaterial

00:51:27.270 --> 00:51:28.763
in such a complex way.

00:51:30.010 --> 00:51:33.603
I see that running through
all of your pieces.

00:51:34.840 --> 00:51:35.850
Because of time,

00:51:35.850 --> 00:51:37.370
I wanna make sure we get questions

00:51:37.370 --> 00:51:38.600
from the audience.

00:51:38.600 --> 00:51:40.700
So before asking any of my own questions,

00:51:40.700 --> 00:51:42.290
cause I can always follow up with you.

00:51:42.290 --> 00:51:43.960
I'd like to see if
anyone from the audience

00:51:43.960 --> 00:51:45.313
would like to start us off.

00:51:47.590 --> 00:51:52.010
I see Michael G. Vann followed by Nam Kim.

00:51:52.010 --> 00:51:54.530
So I'll start with Michael and then follow

00:51:54.530 --> 00:51:55.363
with Nam Kim.

00:51:58.160 --> 00:51:58.993
- Hi, good morning.

00:51:58.993 --> 00:52:00.780
Can you see me or hear me?

00:52:00.780 --> 00:52:02.160
- Yeah, we can hear you great, thanks.

00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:04.160
- Okay, great panel.

00:52:04.160 --> 00:52:06.660
And one of the things that came to mind

00:52:06.660 --> 00:52:10.740
for me was repurposing of landscape

00:52:11.640 --> 00:52:14.150
and also repurposing of memories.

00:52:14.150 --> 00:52:16.900
And I know this is strongest for Vietnam,

00:52:16.900 --> 00:52:19.370
but also happens in Cambodia in Laos,

00:52:19.370 --> 00:52:21.739
as one of the panelists could say

00:52:21.739 --> 00:52:24.310
something about repurposing
these landscape memories

00:52:24.310 --> 00:52:26.990
of war for commercial tourism.

00:52:26.990 --> 00:52:28.310
Particularly a Western market,

00:52:28.310 --> 00:52:30.790
but also greater Asian tourism.

00:52:30.790 --> 00:52:32.253
And thank you, great session.

00:52:41.780 --> 00:52:45.983
- I'd be happy to
respond to that question.

00:52:47.220 --> 00:52:48.890
I was just thinking
about this, this morning

00:52:48.890 --> 00:52:50.543
and my planning for the session.

00:52:52.572 --> 00:52:56.450
So there was a man that I studied with,

00:52:58.840 --> 00:53:03.240
in Pforzheim which is one
of the most bombed places

00:53:03.240 --> 00:53:04.620
on earth.

00:53:04.620 --> 00:53:09.620
And his father, had been a soldier

00:53:09.790 --> 00:53:12.843
during the American Vietnam War.

00:53:13.847 --> 00:53:16.980
And his father had learned
how to dismantle bombs

00:53:16.980 --> 00:53:19.540
during that conflict and
then had taught his son.

00:53:19.540 --> 00:53:20.900
And now his son was working

00:53:20.900 --> 00:53:22.923
in the illegal war scrap trade.

00:53:24.860 --> 00:53:26.480
Because he knew where all
the battlefields were,

00:53:26.480 --> 00:53:29.300
because of his dad's involvement,

00:53:29.300 --> 00:53:31.890
going to those battlefields,

00:53:31.890 --> 00:53:33.760
digging up ordnance and then selling them

00:53:33.760 --> 00:53:34.760
on the black market.

00:53:36.060 --> 00:53:41.060
And he also had a tourism company

00:53:41.240 --> 00:53:43.760
where he would take people around on tours

00:53:43.760 --> 00:53:45.060
of these old battlefields.

00:53:46.920 --> 00:53:51.700
And I think that this account,

00:53:51.700 --> 00:53:56.330
is really compelling
because it highlights for me

00:53:56.330 --> 00:53:59.990
that the tourism, or at
least in my conversations

00:53:59.990 --> 00:54:02.370
with this man, that the tourism was part

00:54:02.370 --> 00:54:07.200
of a positive reclaiming of
a traumatic family history.

00:54:07.200 --> 00:54:11.190
That he was able to
transform it into something

00:54:11.190 --> 00:54:16.090
that helped him and his
kin to give it value.

00:54:16.090 --> 00:54:17.710
That was the sense I had from this man

00:54:17.710 --> 00:54:19.460
and my conversations with this man.

00:54:21.800 --> 00:54:24.210
I mean, I'm sure there are
lots of super critical things

00:54:24.210 --> 00:54:25.850
that one could say about war tourism,

00:54:25.850 --> 00:54:27.090
but this was an example

00:54:27.090 --> 00:54:28.640
in which it felt very positive.

00:54:31.130 --> 00:54:32.680
- I could add just a little bit.

00:54:32.680 --> 00:54:35.020
I went over so I'm gonna stay quiet,

00:54:35.020 --> 00:54:38.990
except that one of the problems
in studying these places

00:54:38.990 --> 00:54:41.820
is that the Vietnamese
militaries on the coast

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:44.840
is still very actively
engaged in some of them.

00:54:44.840 --> 00:54:47.550
And the other problem
has to do with Vietnam's

00:54:47.550 --> 00:54:52.100
ongoing stress with China
and its military relations

00:54:52.100 --> 00:54:53.613
with the United States.

00:54:54.900 --> 00:54:58.320
So and people that I talked
to, they were very careful

00:54:58.320 --> 00:55:00.670
because they didn't really
know what were the political

00:55:00.670 --> 00:55:05.670
valences of talking about
American War destruction,

00:55:06.000 --> 00:55:09.560
given American arms
sales. (Jonathan laughing)

00:55:09.560 --> 00:55:11.600
It's so political.

00:55:11.600 --> 00:55:13.250
I think that's really stalled it.

00:55:18.120 --> 00:55:20.080
- I don't have too much
to say about tourism

00:55:20.080 --> 00:55:23.690
except it's interesting the
way that it for shortens

00:55:25.860 --> 00:55:28.473
the historical processes.

00:55:29.590 --> 00:55:33.736
In Cambodia, the Khmer
Rouge have only just left

00:55:33.736 --> 00:55:35.880
PhnomPenh every single day.

00:55:35.880 --> 00:55:37.210
It was just yesterday, right?

00:55:37.210 --> 00:55:41.020
So tourists show up and
hang out at these cafes

00:55:43.480 --> 00:55:46.013
and watch "Apocalypse Now" and right.

00:55:47.900 --> 00:55:50.760
And I mean, it's
interesting to sort of ways

00:55:50.760 --> 00:55:52.290
that these things distort history

00:55:52.290 --> 00:55:55.410
as opposed to placing the places

00:55:55.410 --> 00:55:57.490
that they are visiting within history.

00:55:57.490 --> 00:56:00.310
So it's not something I've looked at,

00:56:00.310 --> 00:56:05.310
but I did have a tourist
wander by this long house

00:56:05.550 --> 00:56:07.700
that I was doing recording in one time.

00:56:07.700 --> 00:56:09.810
So I think that they probably went home

00:56:09.810 --> 00:56:12.410
with this wonderful story
of how they've gotten

00:56:12.410 --> 00:56:15.660
so authentically into Cambodia
that they actually found

00:56:15.660 --> 00:56:18.450
an anthropologist that worked
up in the hills, right.

00:56:18.450 --> 00:56:20.680
I thought that was
quite a funny encounter.

00:56:20.680 --> 00:56:21.513
So anyway.

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:24.820
- Thank you, everyone.

00:56:24.820 --> 00:56:27.880
We're gonna turn to Nam
Kim who's an archeologist

00:56:27.880 --> 00:56:31.610
from the University of Madison, Wisconsin,

00:56:31.610 --> 00:56:34.030
also great experience in war landscapes,

00:56:34.030 --> 00:56:36.010
but in the archaeological record.

00:56:36.010 --> 00:56:37.280
Go ahead, Nam..

00:56:37.280 --> 00:56:38.113
- Thank you.

00:56:38.113 --> 00:56:39.090
Can you hear me?

00:56:39.090 --> 00:56:39.923
- [Eric] Yes.

00:56:39.923 --> 00:56:41.437
- Okay.

00:56:41.437 --> 00:56:42.860
Many thanks, Eric and
the esteemed panelists

00:56:42.860 --> 00:56:44.930
for a fascinating discussion.

00:56:44.930 --> 00:56:46.420
I have a couple of comments,

00:56:46.420 --> 00:56:48.540
and then a couple of questions
I'm hoping the panelists

00:56:48.540 --> 00:56:50.433
might engage with.

00:56:51.400 --> 00:56:54.070
Each of the panelists gives
us a very powerful reminder

00:56:54.070 --> 00:56:57.250
about how the past and
purported ownership of that past

00:56:57.250 --> 00:57:00.603
can be tied to physical pieces
of terrain and artifacts.

00:57:01.860 --> 00:57:04.540
I found particularly
fascinating, Leah Zani's

00:57:04.540 --> 00:57:07.810
example that piece of
motorcycle being presented

00:57:07.810 --> 00:57:11.340
as a fragment of the war or of a war.

00:57:11.340 --> 00:57:13.480
And it's reminiscent
of how many communities

00:57:13.480 --> 00:57:16.310
produce counterfeit artifacts purportedly

00:57:16.310 --> 00:57:19.220
from the ancient world
for various agendas,

00:57:19.220 --> 00:57:20.053
and for profit.

00:57:21.160 --> 00:57:23.800
I also appreciated David's assertion

00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:26.330
about this presentis bias
and how we need to consider

00:57:26.330 --> 00:57:29.260
the layers of warfare and conflict history

00:57:29.260 --> 00:57:31.840
that interdigitate and become embedded

00:57:31.840 --> 00:57:33.790
in these physical spaces.

00:57:33.790 --> 00:57:35.820
My own work at the site of Goluan

00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:38.900
in Vietnam illustrates how
conflict can leave these kinds

00:57:38.900 --> 00:57:42.843
of scars on the environment
and on terrain over millennia.

00:57:43.690 --> 00:57:45.360
Some of those ramparts
that were constructed

00:57:45.360 --> 00:57:48.720
over 2,000 years ago,
during the 20th century,

00:57:48.720 --> 00:57:51.940
soldiers dug slit trenches
and bunkers right into them.

00:57:51.940 --> 00:57:53.660
And it gives us a kind of illustration

00:57:53.660 --> 00:57:57.910
of how a space is militarized
through the course

00:57:57.910 --> 00:58:00.693
of longer terms of history.

00:58:01.570 --> 00:58:02.930
To relate it to these comments,

00:58:02.930 --> 00:58:05.980
I'm interested to see how
our panelists might view

00:58:05.980 --> 00:58:08.580
these kinds of ethical
questions about ownership

00:58:08.580 --> 00:58:10.710
and presentation of that past,

00:58:10.710 --> 00:58:13.263
given different stakeholders.

00:58:14.470 --> 00:58:16.250
Who says what should be preserved,

00:58:16.250 --> 00:58:18.060
how it should be preserved?

00:58:18.060 --> 00:58:21.230
How we develop narratives
around those remains?

00:58:21.230 --> 00:58:24.100
How these landscapes might
be considered sacred,

00:58:24.100 --> 00:58:27.420
or significant as spaces of commemoration,

00:58:27.420 --> 00:58:29.193
maybe even on a national level?

00:58:30.180 --> 00:58:31.610
So essentially, in your experiences,

00:58:31.610 --> 00:58:33.600
how do communities at both the local

00:58:33.600 --> 00:58:35.810
and national levels work
together to determine

00:58:35.810 --> 00:58:38.120
how these spaces are explained,

00:58:38.120 --> 00:58:40.690
commemorated or exhibited?

00:58:40.690 --> 00:58:41.523
Thank you.

00:58:45.930 --> 00:58:48.507
- Well, I think, one thing that struck me

00:58:48.507 --> 00:58:51.430
and talking about tourism is
that the really popular form

00:58:51.430 --> 00:58:54.510
of tourism in these
villages, and in these sites

00:58:54.510 --> 00:58:57.473
is actually a kind of funerary tourism.

00:58:58.460 --> 00:59:02.420
You see it in the building
of ancestral shrines.

00:59:02.420 --> 00:59:07.420
You see it in Nash state sponsored efforts

00:59:07.450 --> 00:59:12.450
to recognize the dead and to
placate the wandering ghosts.

00:59:14.530 --> 00:59:17.750
And I think this is really interesting

00:59:17.750 --> 00:59:19.330
in a local context,

00:59:19.330 --> 00:59:22.480
how different it might be,
say, in the American West,

00:59:22.480 --> 00:59:24.741
it places like Wounded Knee,

00:59:24.741 --> 00:59:26.610
and other sites like that.

00:59:26.610 --> 00:59:29.560
Or the lynching sites in Mississippi,

00:59:29.560 --> 00:59:31.973
where you have this extreme violence.

00:59:33.380 --> 00:59:35.110
And I think that in Vietnam,

00:59:35.110 --> 00:59:37.300
the discussions about commemoration,

00:59:37.300 --> 00:59:39.520
both on the national and the local level,

00:59:39.520 --> 00:59:42.760
are really much moving in
parallel with those discussions,

00:59:42.760 --> 00:59:44.730
say, in the American West,

00:59:44.730 --> 00:59:48.743
in the American South about
how do you respectfully,

00:59:49.950 --> 00:59:54.950
preserve this kind of really
deep, horrible violence

00:59:57.310 --> 01:00:02.310
without somehow reproducing
it or celebrating it,

01:00:03.120 --> 01:00:05.550
or somehow demeaning it.

01:00:05.550 --> 01:00:07.603
And that's very, very hard.

01:00:14.660 --> 01:00:17.430
- I might just comment that this question

01:00:17.430 --> 01:00:22.430
of what kinds of presentation
of the past are authorized

01:00:25.730 --> 01:00:30.730
or in what ways might
the past that landscapes

01:00:30.970 --> 01:00:33.963
be incorporated into these
practices of commemoration?

01:00:35.240 --> 01:00:38.900
This is one of the sort of enduring themes

01:00:38.900 --> 01:00:43.030
of much writing about
Southeast Asia and certainly

01:00:43.030 --> 01:00:48.030
about the post war in the
mainland Southeast Asia.

01:00:50.500 --> 01:00:53.910
It goes back to Ben
Anderson, for instance,

01:00:53.910 --> 01:00:56.540
on official nationalisms, right,

01:00:56.540 --> 01:00:59.040
this idea that there
are these nationalisms

01:00:59.040 --> 01:01:01.620
that threaten the order,

01:01:01.620 --> 01:01:04.870
and then there are these
efforts to impose official

01:01:04.870 --> 01:01:09.110
versions of national pasts
that would be amenable

01:01:09.110 --> 01:01:14.110
to projects of rule, I suppose.

01:01:14.450 --> 01:01:19.010
And that shows up in sort of different way

01:01:19.010 --> 01:01:22.949
in Hannah Quan's "Ghosts Of War," right.

01:01:22.949 --> 01:01:25.170
That there are certain figures

01:01:25.170 --> 01:01:27.370
from the past that are worthy

01:01:27.370 --> 01:01:29.210
of national remembrance.

01:01:29.210 --> 01:01:32.747
And then you have these
from below processes again,

01:01:32.747 --> 01:01:37.747
that same font of memory,

01:01:38.010 --> 01:01:42.713
that same font of looking
to the past for confirmation

01:01:43.810 --> 01:01:47.320
for bolstering present day purposes,

01:01:47.320 --> 01:01:51.190
can be sort of tapped into for a variety

01:01:51.190 --> 01:01:55.360
of different sort of
approaches that is to say

01:01:55.360 --> 01:01:58.470
that commemoration serves
different political agendas,

01:01:58.470 --> 01:02:03.470
different agendas relating
to identity and belonging

01:02:03.990 --> 01:02:05.490
in the present.

01:02:05.490 --> 01:02:07.930
And that's the case for
landscapes as well, right.

01:02:07.930 --> 01:02:09.170
So I think that's part of the reason

01:02:09.170 --> 01:02:11.020
why I was trying to suggest

01:02:11.020 --> 01:02:13.050
in my book, for instance,

01:02:13.050 --> 01:02:17.900
that this notion of history from the hills

01:02:17.900 --> 01:02:21.930
that this provides alternative
readings of the past.

01:02:21.930 --> 01:02:26.180
And the fact that it's
accessible through the places

01:02:26.180 --> 01:02:28.220
that one would walk through, right.

01:02:28.220 --> 01:02:32.040
That one can actually
inhabit versions of the past

01:02:32.040 --> 01:02:35.320
that speak to your present day reality.

01:02:35.320 --> 01:02:39.440
I think that these are
the kinds of ways I think

01:02:39.440 --> 01:02:44.440
that if we were to try
to look at your question

01:02:44.650 --> 01:02:46.670
from the perspective of
the people who are living

01:02:46.670 --> 01:02:49.060
in these landscapes
you might start getting

01:02:49.060 --> 01:02:51.770
towards some of these kinds of approaches

01:02:51.770 --> 01:02:53.333
to what those landscapes mean.

01:02:57.630 --> 01:03:00.520
- I think that David and Jonathan
have done an excellent job

01:03:00.520 --> 01:03:05.300
of responding to the
larger social implications

01:03:05.300 --> 01:03:07.210
of Nam's question.

01:03:07.210 --> 01:03:12.080
So I would like to respond to his comment

01:03:12.080 --> 01:03:13.240
about interdigitation,

01:03:13.240 --> 01:03:17.013
which is a great word. (Leah laughing)

01:03:18.698 --> 01:03:22.420
And I talk about this in one
of the chapters in my book

01:03:22.420 --> 01:03:23.623
about the gold mine.

01:03:24.670 --> 01:03:29.670
Where these heavily
contaminated parts of Laos

01:03:30.040 --> 01:03:35.040
are shared areas of
interests for archaeologists,

01:03:36.100 --> 01:03:39.380
gold miners, and bomb clearance,

01:03:39.380 --> 01:03:44.380
they share the same soil as
their zone of intervention.

01:03:44.970 --> 01:03:48.770
And all three of these professional people

01:03:48.770 --> 01:03:50.780
are kind of inter trained.

01:03:50.780 --> 01:03:54.390
So for example, if the
gold mine that I studied,

01:03:54.390 --> 01:03:57.260
the gold miners are trained to identify

01:03:59.090 --> 01:04:02.470
possible explosive ordnance,
which they will dig up

01:04:02.470 --> 01:04:05.230
with the gold, and also
archaeological evidence,

01:04:05.230 --> 01:04:07.533
which they will also dig up with the gold.

01:04:09.781 --> 01:04:12.860
And clearance technicians are also trained

01:04:12.860 --> 01:04:17.050
to identify possible
archaeological artifacts,

01:04:17.050 --> 01:04:19.070
and they treat them in much the same way

01:04:19.070 --> 01:04:22.210
that they would treat a
bomb that it is subject

01:04:22.210 --> 01:04:25.210
to the same kind of careful attention

01:04:25.210 --> 01:04:28.963
and with the hands
carefully digging it up.

01:04:29.960 --> 01:04:34.960
And so there's just a really
interesting interdigitation

01:04:35.330 --> 01:04:38.373
of those three practices and
these contaminated areas.

01:04:39.480 --> 01:04:42.010
And that this is not just an accident,

01:04:42.010 --> 01:04:47.010
that the war has this ancillary effect

01:04:48.940 --> 01:04:51.040
of preserving certain areas,

01:04:51.040 --> 01:04:52.550
making it very difficult for people

01:04:52.550 --> 01:04:54.660
to develop those zones.

01:04:54.660 --> 01:04:58.910
And so they then become
sort of resource preserves

01:04:59.780 --> 01:05:03.170
and that's one of the reasons
that Laos was targeted

01:05:03.170 --> 01:05:05.670
by this multinational goldmine company

01:05:05.670 --> 01:05:07.810
because it lost it's gold reserves

01:05:07.810 --> 01:05:10.940
had been preserved by the war.

01:05:10.940 --> 01:05:13.330
And it had been too dangerous to mine

01:05:13.330 --> 01:05:14.950
in Laos for several decades.

01:05:14.950 --> 01:05:16.590
And the same thing goes
for its archeological

01:05:16.590 --> 01:05:17.713
artifacts as well.

01:05:18.746 --> 01:05:21.340
So thinking about not only
how are these processes

01:05:21.340 --> 01:05:24.310
interdigitated for practitioners
for a bomb clearance

01:05:24.310 --> 01:05:26.370
technician who's also on the lookout

01:05:26.370 --> 01:05:28.330
for archaeological artifacts,

01:05:28.330 --> 01:05:31.910
but what historical processes lead to that

01:05:31.910 --> 01:05:33.620
interdigitation over time,

01:05:33.620 --> 01:05:36.960
and how militarism is one
of the forces that causes

01:05:36.960 --> 01:05:40.153
these things to mix.

01:05:49.760 --> 01:05:51.610
- Can I add one little piece to this.

01:05:52.477 --> 01:05:54.210
Your comments Leah remind me

01:05:54.210 --> 01:05:59.210
that there's also a
dimension to preservation

01:05:59.440 --> 01:06:03.040
and excavation here on these sites.

01:06:03.040 --> 01:06:06.770
That is a tension between
local communities in the state

01:06:06.770 --> 01:06:08.710
and Vietnamese communities,

01:06:08.710 --> 01:06:12.100
even patriotic communities who served

01:06:12.100 --> 01:06:15.830
on the winning side, often
are in really strong tension

01:06:15.830 --> 01:06:17.240
with the state.

01:06:17.240 --> 01:06:21.930
Because state actors and
provincial level actors,

01:06:21.930 --> 01:06:26.130
they have looked at other
examples of militarized spaces

01:06:26.130 --> 01:06:28.730
like in Germany after World War Two.

01:06:28.730 --> 01:06:31.710
And there's a certain global dialogue

01:06:31.710 --> 01:06:34.720
about what to do about
so called ruined spaces,

01:06:34.720 --> 01:06:38.940
both in terms of exhuming
bodies, commemorations,

01:06:38.940 --> 01:06:42.510
how to appropriately do that,
the legal ramifications.

01:06:42.510 --> 01:06:46.430
But also the sort of socio
economic transformations.

01:06:46.430 --> 01:06:50.350
And, the interesting thing
about these base footprints

01:06:50.350 --> 01:06:53.190
in Vietnam, is that the base footprints

01:06:53.190 --> 01:06:56.890
are still there, they're
now industrial export zones.

01:06:56.890 --> 01:07:00.030
The same sewer lines,
electric grids, streets,

01:07:00.030 --> 01:07:05.030
and since they took the historic
1960s base architecture,

01:07:06.360 --> 01:07:09.000
which sometimes was
sitting on earlier levels,

01:07:09.000 --> 01:07:11.480
and they just kept it
as an industrial park.

01:07:11.480 --> 01:07:13.920
So the villages never got the land back.

01:07:13.920 --> 01:07:15.500
And so a lot of people in the villages

01:07:15.500 --> 01:07:18.500
are still mad about the land

01:07:18.500 --> 01:07:22.690
that was taken maybe
by the US Army in 1964

01:07:23.540 --> 01:07:27.380
that's now occupied by the
provincial industrial park.

01:07:27.380 --> 01:07:31.500
So there's some
interesting actors involved

01:07:31.500 --> 01:07:34.810
and it's not the worst so
much that's so political

01:07:34.810 --> 01:07:37.840
in central Vietnam, it's
the land and the land values

01:07:37.840 --> 01:07:39.967
and the real estate, and who owns it,

01:07:39.967 --> 01:07:41.630
and who benefits from it?

01:07:41.630 --> 01:07:44.160
That is, in a sense the difficult thing

01:07:44.160 --> 01:07:46.003
about exploring these places.

01:07:47.570 --> 01:07:48.510
- You know I'll just jump in too

01:07:48.510 --> 01:07:50.430
because there's been some comments

01:07:50.430 --> 01:07:52.260
or people have been asking about the sort

01:07:52.260 --> 01:07:54.523
of it's all rubble in ruins, right?

01:07:55.479 --> 01:07:56.640
And I think it's just so interesting

01:07:56.640 --> 01:08:01.640
how these objects from the
past as they break down

01:08:04.620 --> 01:08:06.780
the narratives around them
also break down, right.

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:10.013
So that it's this sort of fragmenting,

01:08:12.803 --> 01:08:15.730
as you watch the claims on the past

01:08:15.730 --> 01:08:17.680
are sort of tied to the integrity

01:08:17.680 --> 01:08:21.340
of the ruins, the landscapes of the past.

01:08:21.340 --> 01:08:23.960
And so we see in David's example

01:08:23.960 --> 01:08:26.850
where even the lone tank from the village

01:08:26.850 --> 01:08:28.720
has been broken down into scrap

01:08:28.720 --> 01:08:31.670
or you look at Leah's great discussions

01:08:31.670 --> 01:08:33.970
of the ways that bombs are repurposed

01:08:33.970 --> 01:08:36.200
and pulled apart, right.

01:08:36.200 --> 01:08:39.430
And this really ties into
if any of you have read

01:08:39.430 --> 01:08:42.617
this amazing book by Kim Fortuin

01:08:42.617 --> 01:08:44.717
"Advocacy after BhoPal."

01:08:45.650 --> 01:08:50.380
Her point is that the Indian government

01:08:50.380 --> 01:08:53.100
was very interested to get
rid of the narrative, right.

01:08:53.100 --> 01:08:56.140
Bhopal ended there was an end to the past

01:08:56.140 --> 01:08:59.420
and what these ruins and rubble bring

01:08:59.420 --> 01:09:04.420
on right in this sort
of, and Laura Stollerway

01:09:05.070 --> 01:09:07.000
are sort of if you think
about some of the writing

01:09:07.000 --> 01:09:09.380
about rubble and ruin recently.

01:09:09.380 --> 01:09:12.290
That the breaking down of these pieces

01:09:12.290 --> 01:09:16.270
of the past is accompanied by a process

01:09:16.270 --> 01:09:19.610
of trying to de legitimize
claims made on the basis

01:09:19.610 --> 01:09:20.730
of a shared experience

01:09:20.730 --> 01:09:21.570
of the past, right.

01:09:21.570 --> 01:09:26.270
That there's also a
community of Kim Fortuin

01:09:26.270 --> 01:09:29.150
calls it a community of voicing the past

01:09:29.150 --> 01:09:33.390
that is under attack from,
right those who are opposed

01:09:33.390 --> 01:09:38.390
to whatever the political
goal of bringing out

01:09:38.600 --> 01:09:39.910
that past is all about.

01:09:39.910 --> 01:09:43.510
So it's just interesting to
see how there's connections

01:09:43.510 --> 01:09:46.540
among all the different
pieces of the writings

01:09:46.540 --> 01:09:49.400
I think your your pile
of books on your desk,

01:09:49.400 --> 01:09:52.150
Eric was absolutely in
conversation with each other.

01:09:52.150 --> 01:09:54.870
So I just find it interesting

01:09:54.870 --> 01:09:57.180
to see that these themes
are cutting across

01:09:57.180 --> 01:09:59.483
all of our discussions and questions.

